## Before the first run
1. Lift the robot so the wheels turn freely. Power the driver (24–48 V) and plug the USB–RS485 adapter into the computer
   (A→A, B→B, GND). `sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER`, then log out and in once.
2. Find the port: `ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/`
3. Check the driver without ROS:
   ```bash
   python3 src/tesr_zlac_hardware/tools/zlac_check.py /dev/ttyUSB1             # finds node ids, shows faults and speed
   python3 src/tesr_zlac_hardware/tools/zlac_check.py /dev/ttyUSB1 --spin 20   # both wheels 20 rpm for 3 s
   ```
   Both wheels must roll the robot **forward**. The right motor is mounted mirrored, so `invert` is `true` for the
   right wheels by default — if a wheel turns the wrong way, flip its `invert` in
   `src/{{ r.package("description") }}/urdf/ros2_control.xacro`.
4. Run: `ros2 launch {{ r.package("bringup") }} robot.launch.py hardware:=real motor_port:=/dev/ttyUSB1`

## What the plugin does
- on start: clears faults, velocity mode (0x200D = 3), acceleration/deceleration {{ p.real_params.accel_ms | default(300) }}/{{ p.real_params.decel_ms | default(300) }} ms,
  communication watchdog {{ p.real_params.watchdog_ms | default(500) }} ms (the driver stops the motors when the computer goes quiet), enable (0x200E = 8)
- every cycle: target speed in rpm (0x2088/0x2089), then faults, encoder counts and speed (0x20A5–0x20AC) in one read
- odometry position integrates the measured speed; set the hardware parameter `counts_per_rev` to use the encoder counts instead
- stop: target 0, control word 7. Faults are printed by name (over-current, Hall sensor error …).

The register map follows the ZLAC8015D RS485 Communication Instruction V1.04. Checked against a simulated driver on a
virtual serial port, not yet on a physical driver — report anything odd to TESR (LINE www.tesrshop.com/line).
